      "correct": 0,
      "explanation": "Correct: (A) 85 m/s toward the east. Each wavefront is centered where the source was when it was emitted, so wavefronts bunch up on the side the source moves toward: east. In front, λ<sub>front</sub> = (v − v<sub>s</sub>)/f; behind, λ<sub>back</sub> = (v + v<sub>s</sub>)/f. Their ratio gives (340 − v<sub>s</sub>)/(340 + v<sub>s</sub>) = 0.60/1.00, so 340 − v<sub>s</sub> = 204 + 0.60v<sub>s</sub>, 136 = 1.60v<sub>s</sub>, and v<sub>s</sub> = 85 m/s. The emitted frequency is (340 − 85)/0.60 = 425 Hz. (B) is wrong because the source moves toward the side where the wavefronts are crowded, because each new wavefront starts closer to the ones ahead of it. (C) is wrong because it uses v<sub>s</sub> = v(1 − 0.60/1.00) = 136 m/s, which treats the back spacing as the unshifted wavelength; behind the source the wavelength is stretched too. (D) is wrong because an observer's motion does not change the wavefronts in the air; an uneven spacing like this can only come from a moving source.",
