      "explanation": "Correct: (B) 10 Hz. A string fixed at both ends has a node at each end, so the fundamental (first harmonic) fits exactly half a wavelength on the string: L = λ/2, so λ₁ = 2L = 2(3.0 m) = 6.0 m. Using v = fλ, the frequency is f₁ = v/λ₁ = v/(2L) = (60 m/s)/(6.0 m) = 10 Hz. The general rule is f<sub>n</sub> = nv/(2L), so every higher harmonic is a whole-number multiple of this 10 Hz fundamental. Units check: (m/s)/m = 1/s = Hz.\n\n(A) is wrong because 5.0 Hz comes from v/(4L) = 60/12 = 5.0 Hz, the formula for a pipe closed at one end, which does not apply to a string with nodes at both ends.\n(C) is wrong because 20 Hz comes from v/L = 60/3.0 = 20 Hz, which treats the string length as a full wavelength instead of half a wavelength for the fundamental.\n(D) is wrong because 180 Hz comes from multiplying v × L = 60 × 3.0 = 180, which gives units of m²/s rather than Hz; frequency is speed divided by wavelength."
